## Key Points to Remember

```text
1. First count k nodes.
2. If count < k → don't reverse.
3. If count == k → reverse exactly k nodes.
4. Use the standard reversal pattern.
5. `temp` stores the next group.
6. Recursively process the remaining list.
7. Connect using `head.next`.
8. Return `pre` because it becomes the new head.
```

---

## Most Important Reversal Pattern

```java
temp = curr.next;
curr.next = pre;
pre = curr;
curr = temp;
```

Remember:

```text
SAVE → REVERSE → MOVE PRE → MOVE CURR
```

---

## Complexity

### Time Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

Every node is processed once.

### Space Complexity

```text
O(n/k)
```

because of the recursive call stack.

Worst case:

```text
O(n)
```

---

## Interview Explanation

> First, I check whether there are at least `k` nodes available. If there are `k` nodes, I reverse exactly those nodes using `pre`, `curr`, and `temp`. After reversing the current group, I recursively process the remaining list and connect it using `head.next`. If fewer than `k` nodes remain, I leave them unchanged.

## Revision Formula

```text
Check k
   ↓
Reverse k
   ↓
Recursive remaining list
   ↓
Connect
   ↓
Return new head
```
